# Incident: 2026-04-08 20-44-05

## Summary

Between the hour of 20:44 UTC and 20:48 UTC on 2026-04-08, 12 users encountered unable to place pizza orders due to chaos testing. The event was triggered by chaos testing at 20:44 UTC. The call to the `/chaos` endpoint triggered the chaos testing failure mode.

The event was detected by Grafana IRM and notified the on-call team member via push notification and SMS at 20:46 UTC. The team  acknowledged the alert and started working on the event by 20:47 UTC, being resolved by 20:48 UTC. This high-severity incident affected 100% of users during the four minute window between detection and resolution.


## Detection

This incident was detected when the `jwt-pizza-service` Chaos Testing alert was triggered and paged the on-call team member (Josh Breiter). Logs on the JWT Pizza Service dashboard corroborated the failure caused by chaos testing.

The detection threshold was tuned low enough to enable quick detection and remediation of the issue.

## Impact

For 4 minutes between 20:44 UTC and 20:48 UTC on 04/08/26, all pizza order creation requests failed for the 12 users active during the incident window.

No support tickets were opened as part of this incident.

## Timeline

All times are UTC.

- 20:44:05 - External chaos monkey calls the `/chaos` endpoint and initiates the chaos testing failure
- 20:46 - Enough failures have accumulated to trigger the 1 minute failure accumulation threshold
- 20:46 - Alert monitoring detects the 500 error on the `/api/order` path and detects the "Chaos monkey" string in the log content
- 20:46 - Alert is dispatched to on-call team member via Grafana IRM
- 20:47 - Alert was ackowledged by Josh Breiter
- 20:48 - Using error logs, the failure mode was identified
- 20:48 - The reportUrl contained in the factory error log was visited to stop the chaos test (https://pizza-factory.cs329.click/api/support/d231864fc7f04558bc34e28866150374/report/1op809se)
- 20:52 - After monitoring for further events and placing a test order without issue, the on-call engineer determines that the issue has been resolved and marks it resolved in Grafana IRM.
- 21:25 - Incident report published to GitHub.
```

## Response

After receiving a page at 20:46 UTC, Josh Breiter came online at 20:48 UTC in Grafana. Upon examining the error responses, the reportUrl embedded in the response was visited. 

After visiting the URL, further monitoring of the system and a test order was placed to ensure that the system was functioning correctly.

```

## Root cause

The chaos testing endpoint was called by an external individual with access to the admin account.

## Resolution

By visting the embedded report URL, the chaos testing was stopped. This signaled to the factory that the chaos event had been ackowledged, after which the system returned to normal operation.

## Prevention

To prevent this in the future, the admin password should be rotated on a regular basis. In addition to that, additional restrictions will be placed around who can initiate chaos testing. 

## Action items

1. Rotate admin password on a regular basis
2. Place addtional restrictions on chaos testing endpoint to avoid accidentally invoking it
